<Page
  title="How We Research Volleyball Gear"
  description="Where the specs on VolleyballTech come from, how we choose products, what we have and have not tested, and how affiliate links fit in."
>
  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <p>
    VolleyballTech is run by a recreational player, not a lab. Most product pages on this site are based on
    research, not on our own testing, and every page says which of the two it is. We would rather be clear about
    that than pretend.
  </p>

  <h2>Where our information comes from</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Manufacturer information.</strong> Spec sheets, product pages and launch announcements from the brand are our first source.</li>
    <li><strong>Retailer listings.</strong> We compare several stores to confirm sizes, materials, versions and list prices.</li>
    <li><strong>Published buyer feedback.</strong> We read reviews to find patterns, such as a shoe that runs narrow or a pad that runs small. One complaint is not a pattern.</li>
    <li><strong>Rule books and official bodies.</strong> Ball sizes, net heights and approvals come from FIVB, USA Volleyball, NCAA and NFHS material.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When sources disagree, we say so on the page. When we cannot confirm a number, we leave it out.
  </p>

  <h2>What our labels mean</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Research-based, not yet tested hands-on</strong> means we have not used the product ourselves. The page explains what the product is, who it suits and who should skip it, based on the sources above.</li>
    <li><strong>Tested hands-on</strong> means we have used the product on a court. These pages will carry our own notes and photos.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we choose products</h2>
  <p>
    We cover products that players actually ask about: current models from the main volleyball brands, popular
    budget options, and gear that solves a specific problem. Every page has a "who should skip it" section,
    because a product that is right for a college middle blocker is often wrong for a twelve-year-old beginner.
  </p>

  <h2>Price ranges instead of prices</h2>
  <p>
    Prices for sports gear change every week and differ by size and color. We show a rough price range ($, $$ or
    $$$) based on list prices and link to the store, where you can see today's price.
  </p>

  <h2>Affiliate links</h2>
  <p>
    Buttons that say "Check price on Amazon" are affiliate links. If you buy through them, we may earn a
    commission at no extra cost to you. As an Amazon Associate we earn from qualifying purchases. This pays for
    the site. It does not decide which products we cover or how we describe them. Read more in our
    <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Health and injuries</h2>
  <p>
    We explain what knee pads and ankle braces are designed to do. We are not doctors. If you are injured or
    coming back from an injury, talk to a doctor, physical therapist or athletic trainer about what you need.
  </p>

  <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Product pages show the date of the last update. Brands change models often, so we re-check pages regularly.
    If you find a mistake, we want to hear about it and will correct it.
  </p>
</Page>
